-- title:
--   Recursion (5) for availability, per network
-- statement:
--   Let $q^s$ be feasible for Problem (2) and $c$ a network of $q^s$, with $N_c$ locations and workload $M_c=\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A_c}\Lambda_{ij}q^s_{ij}/\mu_{ij}$. Let $(K_c)$ and $(K'_c)$ be allocations that agree outside $c$, with $K_c=K'_c+1$ in $c$. Let $\pi$ be a stationary distribution of the static chain with $K+1$ vehicles concentrated on allocation $(K_c)$, and $\pi'$ one of the static chain with $K$ vehicles concentrated on $(K'_c)$. Then for every location $i$ of $c$
--
--   $$
--   \alpha_i(\pi)=\frac{K_c}{N_c+K_c-1+\big(1-\alpha_i(\pi')\big)M_c}.
--   $$
--
--   This is the relation $\alpha(q^s,K_c,N_c)=K_c/(N_c+K_c-1+(1-\alpha(q^s,K_c-1,N_c))M_c)$ on p. 18; for a single network it is equation (5) (p. 8), proved in the Appendix from the random observer property and Little's law. It relates the availability with $K_c$ vehicles in network $c$ to that with $K_c-1$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - reading: α(q^s,Kc,Nc) and α(q^s,Kc−1,Nc), the availability of network c with Kc and Kc − 1 vehicles → avail π i and avail π' i at a location i of c, with π a stationary law of the static chain with K+1 vehicles on allocation Kc and π' one with K vehicles on allocation Kc' (Kc' = Kc − 1 on c, Kc' = Kc elsewhere); reason: the paper's α is the availability Σ_{x:x_i>0} π(x) (p. 6), common to the locations of c; the other networks' allocations do not affect it, so any common values are allowed
--   - reading: networks 𝒩_c with N_c locations and M_c = Σ_{(i,j)∈𝒜_c} (Λij/μij) q^s_ij → connected components of the flow graph of q^s; Nc its number of locations; Mc summed over the pairs (i,j) with i in c; reason: Reading 4; pairs without flow contribute 0 to Mc
--   - generalization: q^s optimal for Problem (2) with Δ ≥ K → q^s feasible for Problem (2), any Δ; reason: only the bounds 0 ≤ q^s ≤ 1 and balanced flows are used; an optimal q^s is feasible
--   - generalization: q^s induces one strongly connected network (p. 6; Section 3) → q^s induces any number of networks; the statement is per network; reason: Section 6 relaxes the assumption; a single network is the case of one component
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, proof of Theorem 4, p. 18; eq. (5), p. 8; Appendix, pp. 23–24

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsFeasible2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_ConcentratedOn
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_avail
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_Nc
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_MultiNet_Mc

namespace VehShare.MultiNet

/-- **The recursion (5), per network.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), proof of Theorem 4, p. 18: "`α(q^s, K_c, N_c) = K_c/(N_c + K_c − 1 + (1 − α(q^s, K_c − 1, N_c))M_c)`";
for one network this is (5), p. 8, proved in the Appendix, pp. 23–24.

Let `q^s` be feasible for Problem (2) and `c` a network of `q^s`. Let `(K_c)` and `(K'_c)` be
allocations that agree off `c`, with `K_c = K'_c + 1` in `c`. Let `π` be a stationary distribution of the
static chain with `K + 1` vehicles concentrated on allocation `K`, and `π'` one of the static chain
with `K` vehicles concentrated on allocation `K'`. Then for every location `i` of `c`
`α_i(π) = K_c / (N_c + K_c − 1 + (1 − α_i(π')) M_c)`.

**Formalization Note.** The two systems are stated explicitly (`K + 1` and `K` vehicles, so no
natural-number subtraction). Every quantity is cast to `ℝ` before `− 1`. The denominator is positive
(`N_c ≥ 1`, `K_c ≥ 1`, `α ≤ 1`, `M_c ≥ 0`). Only feasibility of `q^s` is used, with any `Δ`. -/
theorem availability_recursion (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ) (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ)
    (hqs : IsFeasible2 P Δ qs) (Kc Kc' : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent → ℕ)
    (c : (flowGraph P qs).ConnectedComponent) (hcc : Kc c = Kc' c + 1)
    (hoff : ∀ d, d ≠ c → Kc d = Kc' d)
    (π : State P (K + 1) → ℝ) (hπ : IsStationary (staticPolicy P (K + 1) qs hqs.1) π)
    (hπc : ConcentratedOn P qs Kc π)
    (π' : State P K → ℝ) (hπ' : IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π')
    (hπ'c : ConcentratedOn P qs Kc' π')
    (i : P.L) (hi : (flowGraph P qs).connectedComponentMk i = c) :
    avail π i = (Kc c : ℝ) / ((Nc P qs c : ℝ) + (Kc c : ℝ) - 1 + (1 - avail π' i) * Mc P qs c) := by sorry

end VehShare.MultiNet
